---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const mono = "font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.92em; color: var(--text);";
---
<Site title="Free tools"
      description="Small free tools from the makers of Chili RAW. The xmp to .cube converter turns a Lightroom preset into a LUT, on a Mac or on Windows.">
  <div class="wrap" style="padding-top: clamp(40px, 5vw, 64px);">
    <div class="eyebrow">free tools</div>
    <h1 class="h-hero" style="margin-top: 16px;">Free tools</h1>
    <div class="h-sub" style="font-weight: 500; color: var(--dim); margin-top: 14px; max-width: 30ch;">
      Small things we built for ourselves, free for anyone.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap section" style="padding-bottom: 70px;">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="eyebrow">lightroom plugin</div>
        <h3>xmp to .cube converter</h3>
        <p>
          Turns a Lightroom preset into a LUT. Point it at your <span style={mono}>.xmp</span> presets
          and it writes <span style={mono}>.cube</span> files you can use in Chili RAW — or in Resolve,
          Photomator, or anything else that reads a LUT.
        </p>
        <p>It runs wherever Lightroom does, Windows included, and it costs nothing.</p>
        <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top: auto; padding-top: 18px;">
          <a class="btn btn-primary btn-wide" href="https://github.com/halebop17/xmp-to-cube-converter"
             rel="noopener">Get it on GitHub →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="eyebrow">what it carries</div>
        <h3>What a lookup table carries</h3>
        <p>
          A LUT is a lookup table, so the colour in a preset comes across exactly. What does not is
          anything that reads the pixels around it — clarity, sharpening, grain, noise reduction.
          Those belong to the photograph rather than to the look, and Chili RAW has its own.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Site>
